# Accepted memcheck reports for the taint harness. Each entry is a
# secret-dependent access or branch that the design documents and accepts;
# scripts/ct-taint.sh fails on anything not listed here. Add an entry only
# with a reason. Frame names are demangled Rust symbols, matched as globs.
#
# The typed entry points report nothing: `permute` takes an in-domain
# `Symbol`, and `OreEncrypt` and the string encryptors get their symbols from
# the 6-bit decomposition, which masks rather than checks.
#
# The one accepted report is `Blocks6::check`, on the raw-block entry points
# (`OreCipher::encrypt`/`encrypt_left` for Bit6, `encrypt_var`/
# `encrypt_left_var` for the chained scheme). A caller of those passes symbols
# that may be out of the 6-bit domain, and the function must refuse them. It
# ORs every symbol's high bits together without branching and branches once
# on the result, so the branch reveals only whether some symbol was out of
# domain. For a well-formed input it always goes the same way. `check` is
# `#[inline(never)]`, so the branch is always in its own frame, whose name is
# `ore_rs::scheme::decompose::Blocks6<B>::check` (or its v0-mangled form, which
# also contains `Blocks6` and `check`).

{
   blocks6-check: one branch on whether any raw symbol is out of domain
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*Blocks6*check*
}
